<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Documents/ over the USB cable: how the points database, the route
	     and the recorded rides get off the phone without a share sheet. -->
	<key>UIFileSharingEnabled</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- THE EXPORT-COMPLIANCE ANSWER, GIVEN ONCE HERE INSTEAD OF BY HAND ON
	     EVERY UPLOAD. Without this key App Store Connect stops each build at
	     "Missing Compliance" and waits for somebody to answer the same two
	     questions in a web form; with it the build goes straight to
	     processing.

	     AND `NO` IS THE TRUE ANSWER, not the convenient one. The question is
	     about NON-EXEMPT encryption, and the only cryptography anywhere near
	     this app is HTTPS in `PlaceLink.resolve` — the standard TLS of the
	     operating system's own URLSession, used to fetch, which Category 5
	     Part 2 note 4 exempts outright. There is no encryption of the
	     rider's data, no key, no custom algorithm: the points, the route and
	     the rides are written to `Documents/` as plain XML and plain text. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<!-- `pippin://place?lat=…&lon=…&name=…&address=…` (P14).

	     NOT a public interface anybody else is invited to call: it exists
	     so the share extension, which is a different process with a
	     different bundle id, can hand the app a place it has just parsed.
	     See `PlaceLink.callbackURL(for:)` for why the payload travels in a
	     URL rather than through an App Group container.

	     Whatever arrives here is treated as input and not as instruction —
	     `MapScreen.onOpenURL` validates it and the worst a malformed one
	     can do is nothing. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>org.peregrine.Pippin.place</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Editor</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>pippin</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!-- Where "Report a Problem" sends a saved report (P22).

	     AN ICLOUD **HIDE MY EMAIL** ALIAS, not a real mailbox: it can be
	     rotated the day it starts collecting spam, and a rider reading it
	     in their own Mail composer learns nothing about who they are
	     writing to. It lives here rather than in the Swift so it is one
	     line to change, and it is deliberately NOT a service endpoint or
	     an API key — the transport is the rider's own mail app, so this
	     binary carries no credential anybody could extract from the .ipa.

	     LEAVE IT EMPTY AND THE FEATURE STILL WORKS: the Send button turns
	     itself off, the sheet says why, and the share button still gets a
	     report off the phone. See `ReportMail` in Sharing.swift.

	     IT IS A BUILD SETTING AND NOT A LITERAL, because this file is public
	     and an address written here would be scraped within a day of the
	     push. `$(PP_REPORT_ADDRESS)` is declared empty in the tracked
	     `Pippin.xcconfig` and filled in by a git-ignored `local/Local.xcconfig`
	     on the machine that ships — so a clone builds the handled empty
	     state above, and the address never reaches the repository. An
	     undefined build setting expands to the empty string, which is the
	     same handled state again. -->
	<key>PPReportAddress</key>
	<string>$(PP_REPORT_ADDRESS)</string>
</dict>
</plist>
